# What makes a C library's read of freed bytes show in the `run` suite
# (defect 321; panel 190's ffi-pragmatist, §§ 2, 3, 19 and 27 of its report).
#
# A binding whose `lent` is false of its function lets C keep a `str`'s bytes
# past their release, as SQLite keeps a text bound with no destructor, and C
# reads them later. AddressSanitizer does not see that read: the library is not
# instrumented, and ASan's quarantine keeps the freed bytes as they were, so
# the program prints the right answer at exit 0 under `--sanitize`, measured by
# the sitting on this Mac, on Linux arm64 under two clangs and on the Windows
# box. The plain runs see it only by the allocator's accident: Darwin's
# allocator zeroes a small block it frees, so the read turns to zeros there,
# and glibc's leaves the text in place, so it is right by accident there. A
# compiler that releases a string earlier, at its last use or by sharing one
# slot between two arms, would pass every leg the suite had.
#
# Two instruments see it, and this module gives `suite_run.hero` both:
#
# - **ASan's own fill**: `max_free_fill_size` and `free_fill_byte` make ASan
#   write a byte over everything it frees, so the read in C returns that byte
#   and the program prints a wrong answer the expectation refuses. It is given
#   to every sanitised run, so it costs no build. Measured 2026-10-07 on this
#   Mac: the planted keeper below prints 412 under `--sanitize` alone and 680
#   with the fill, eight bytes of 85.
# - **Guard Malloc** on Darwin, `libgmalloc`: each block on a page of its own
#   and a freed one unmapped, so the read faults where it is made. It runs the
#   `-O0` binary the first leg already built. Its own messages go to a file
#   beside the case (`MALLOC_LOG_FILE`) and are told with a failure, since a
#   case's stderr is part of its expectation.
#
# Both reach the program through `env`, the one way to give a child a variable
# that the runtime's spawn has (it passes this process's environment on and
# takes none of its own). Guard Malloc needs it for a second reason: a program
# protected by System Integrity Protection, `/bin/sh` among them, is started
# with every `DYLD_` variable dropped, and `env` is given the variable as an
# argument and sets it itself for the program it starts.
#
# **Where each runs** is a premise about the world, written so it fails
# loudly: Guard Malloc is Darwin's alone, asked of the file Darwin ships it as,
# and a Darwin that does not have it is a failure of the leg, never a leg left
# out in silence (`guard_malloc`). The fill is ASan's on every platform where
# `--sanitize` runs. Measured on this Mac only: the fill on Linux and Windows,
# and `env` on the Windows box, are the round's legs to run.

use shell
use strings

# What every sanitised run of a case is handed: everything ASan frees is
# overwritten with 85 (`U`) at its whole length, the length being the largest
# the option takes in a 32-bit word, so no block is left half as it was.
constant FILL: str
    "ASAN_OPTIONS=max_free_fill_size=2147483647:free_fill_byte=85"

# Where Darwin keeps Guard Malloc, and a file every Darwin has, which is how
# this module asks whether it is on one.
constant GUARD_MALLOC: str
    "/usr/lib/libgmalloc.dylib"

constant DARWIN: str
    "/System/Library/CoreServices/SystemVersion.plist"

# `words` run with the fill handed to the program they start.
function filled(words: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ ["env", FILL]

    for word in words
        out @ out.push(word)

    return out

# Guard Malloc's library where this is Darwin; `not_darwin` elsewhere; and
# `missing` on a Darwin without it, which the leg reports as its failure.
function guard_malloc() -> str?
    if !shell.exists(DARWIN)
        return fail(code: "not_darwin", msg: "Guard Malloc is Darwin's")

    if !shell.exists(GUARD_MALLOC)
        return fail(
            code: "missing",
            msg: "this is Darwin and " + GUARD_MALLOC + " is not here, so no leg of `run` sees a C library read freed bytes on this machine"
        )
    return ok(GUARD_MALLOC)

# `binary` run under Guard Malloc, its messages written to `log`.
function guarded(binary: str, library: str, log: str) -> [str]
    return ["env", "DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=" + library, "MALLOC_LOG_FILE=" + log, binary]

## Tests

# The planted control: a C library of the program's own that keeps the
# pointer it is lent, as SQLite keeps a text bound with no destructor, and
# reads eight of its bytes after the string is freed. Its reader is not
# instrumented, as a system library's is not. The right answer is the sum of
# `"0123456789".repeat(10)`'s bytes 40 to 47, 412.
constant KEEPER_H: str
    "#include <stdint.h>\nstatic const unsigned char *keeper_kept;\nstatic inline void keeper_keep(const char *text) { keeper_kept = (const unsigned char *)text; }\n__attribute__((no_sanitize(\"address\")))\nstatic inline int64_t keeper_sum(int64_t from, int64_t to) {\n    int64_t sum = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = from; i < to; i++) sum += keeper_kept[i];\n    return sum;\n}\n"

# The binding says `lent`, which is false of `keeper_keep`: that is the
# program's mistake, and the one no compiler can see.
constant KEEPER: str
    "extern \"keeper.h\"\n    function keeper_keep(text: cstr lent)\n    function keeper_sum(from: i64, to: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction lend(n: i64)\n    text = \"0123456789\".repeat(10) + n.to_str()\n    keeper_keep(text: text.cstr())\n\nfunction main()\n    lend(7)\n    print(keeper_sum(from: 40, to: 48))\n"

# The same library read while the string is alive: the correct program, which
# every leg must pass, so that what turns the control red is the read of freed
# bytes and nothing else the leg does.
constant READ_IN_TIME: str
    "extern \"keeper.h\"\n    function keeper_keep(text: cstr lent)\n    function keeper_sum(from: i64, to: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction main()\n    text = \"0123456789\".repeat(10) + 7.to_str()\n    keeper_keep(text: text.cstr())\n    print(keeper_sum(from: 40, to: 48))\n"

# The control and its twin, laid out under `dir`.
function planted(dir: str) -> ()?
    shell.mkdir_all(dir)?
    write_file(path: dir + "/keeper.h", text: KEEPER_H)?
    write_file(path: dir + "/keeper.hero", text: KEEPER)?
    write_file(path: dir + "/in_time.hero", text: READ_IN_TIME)?
    return ok()

test "the fill turns a C library's read of freed bytes into a wrong answer, and ASan alone does not (defect 321)"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    dir = scratch + "/freed-321"
    assert !planted(dir).is_err()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()

    # ASan alone: the right answer, and nothing said. This is the blindness.
    alone = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: compiler,
        words: [compiler, "run", dir + "/keeper.hero", "--sanitize", "-O0"]
    ).must()
    assert alone.code == 0
    assert alone.out == "412\n"
    assert !strings.contains(text: alone.err, needle: "AddressSanitizer")

    # With the fill, the bytes C reads are 85 each.
    words = filled([compiler, "run", dir + "/keeper.hero", "--sanitize", "-O0"])
    seen = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: words[0], words: words).must()
    assert seen.out == "680\n"

    # And the program that reads in time is right under the fill too.
    fine = filled([compiler, "run", dir + "/in_time.hero", "--sanitize", "-O0"])
    right = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: fine[0], words: fine).must()
    assert right.code == 0
    assert right.out == "412\n"
    assert right.err == ""
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "Guard Malloc stops a C library at its read of freed bytes, on Darwin (defect 321)"
    library = guard_malloc()

    # Off Darwin there is nothing to run; on a Darwin without it, the leg's
    # own failure is the answer, and so is this test's.
    match library
        .ok => _ = 0
        .err e =>
            assert e.code == "not_darwin"
            return

    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    dir = scratch + "/guarded-321"
    assert !planted(dir).is_err()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()

    for name in ["keeper", "in_time"]
        built = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: compiler,
            words: [compiler, "build", dir + "/" + name + ".hero", "-o", dir + "/" + name + shell.exe_suffix()]
        ).must()
        assert built.code == 0

    stopped = guarded(binary: dir + "/keeper", library: library.must(), log: dir + "/keeper.gmalloc.txt")
    faulted = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: stopped[0], words: stopped).must()
    assert faulted.code != 0
    assert faulted.out == ""
    clean = guarded(binary: dir + "/in_time", library: library.must(), log: dir + "/in_time.gmalloc.txt")
    passed = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: clean[0], words: clean).must()
    assert passed.code == 0
    assert passed.out == "412\n"
    assert passed.err == ""
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()
